Excoriation of resolving insect bites leading to secondary mild inflammatory changes from scratching
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Focal linear abrasions, Minute serosanguinous crusts, Localized pruritus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-007
Reason for Visit: New onset localized pain, erythema, and epidermal breakdown requiring assessment to exclude evolving cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted localized erythema and epidermal disruption following an insect bite. Urgent clinical evaluation was mandated to differentiate benign inflammatory response secondary to mechanical excoriation from an early, spreading bacterial cellulitis prior to the next in-hours period, preventing potential systemic involvement.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I scratched some bug bites raw, and now the area is red and weeping.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports focal linear epidermal abrasions concentrated over an initial papular lesion from suspected mosquito bites 3 days ago. Describes intense pruritus directly localized to the disrupted skin. Notes the development of minute serosanguinous crusts at the site of excoriation. Reports localized mild erythema without significant induration. Lack of streaking erythema or lymphangitis.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Potential for ongoing mechanical trauma due to uncontrolled pruritus.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, focused on the localized skin lesions.
Physical Exam: Skin exam reveals focal linear abrasions, minute serosanguinous crusts, and localized pruritus corresponding to the diagnostic triad. Surrounding localized mild erythema is present, but lacks significant induration or warmth. Petechiae localized to the immediate scratch tract. Absence of fluctuance or abscess formation. Normal localized skin temperature. Normal regional lymph nodes. Bedside ultrasound (if required) would be negative for fluid collection.
Environmental Observations: No obvious indoor insect infestation noted.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current antibiotic or immunosuppressant use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Excoriation of resolving insect bites leading to secondary mild inflammatory changes from scratching - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Early Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of expanding erythema, absence of localized heat, and normal regional lymph nodes.

Cutaneous Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of palpable fluctuance or focal induration.

Herpes Zoster - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of dermatomal distribution and lack of grouped vesicles on an erythematous base.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acutely inflamed, weeping skin lesions following mechanical trauma to an insect bite. The clinical picture mimics early cellulitis, demanding a prompt, face-to-face evaluation. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period carried an unacceptable risk of allowing an occult bacterial infection to spread unchecked. Careful physical examination confirmed mechanical excoriation rather than infectious cellulitis, safely avoiding unnecessary empiric antibiotics and facilitating appropriate symptomatic relief.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansing of the area with antiseptic wash (chlorhexidine). Application of a topical antihistamine/anti-pruritic lotion (e.g., calamine or crotamiton). Short course of oral antihistamines at night to aid sleep and reduce nocturnal scratching.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep fingernails trimmed short and filed smooth to minimize skin damage during involuntary scratching, and apply a cool compress instead of scratching when the itch triggers.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the area becomes increasingly painful or red.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if red streaks develop extending from the bites towards the heart, or if accompanied by high fever, confusion, or lethargy.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; superficial abrasions remain at risk for secondary infection if not kept clean.
Clinical Course: The acute inflammation and itching should subside within 3-5 days provided the skin barrier is protected from further trauma.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the redness spreads rapidly, if pus begins to drain, or if the area becomes extremely painful to touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The redness is caused by physical trauma from fingernails, not an infection. Oral antihistamines reduce the allergic itch response, allowing the skin to heal.
Actionable Steps: Wash the area gently with soap and water daily. Apply cool packs to numb the itch. Do not pick at the crusts.

